 ## Postgres and scaling
 
-The default world-model backend is **SQLite on a ReadWriteOnce volume**. Move
-the world model to Postgres for managed database HA, pooling, and DB-enforced
-tenant isolation:
+The default world-model backend is **SQLite on a ReadWriteOnce volume**.
+Postgres can provide managed database HA, pooling, DB-enforced tenant isolation,
+and the same application-level sealing for sensitive world-model text. Supply a
+common encryption key from a KMS/secret manager to every replica; do not rely on
+the node-local auto-generated key. Database/storage encryption is still required
+as defense in depth:
 
 ```bash
 helm install maverick ./deploy/helm/maverick \
   --set worldModel.backend=postgres \
   --set worldModel.postgres.rls=true \
+  --set-string env.MAVERICK_ENCRYPT_AT_REST=1 \
+  --set-string secret.data.MAVERICK_ENCRYPTION_KEY=*** \
+  --set-string secret.data.MAVERICK_ENCRYPTION_KEY_DIGEST=sha256:*** \
   --set-string secret.data.MAVERICK_PG_DSN=postgres://maverick:***@host:5432/maverick \
   --set-string secret.data.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
 ```
 
-Keep `replicaCount=1` and `autoscaling.enabled=false`. Postgres centralizes the
-world model, but workflow releases/runs, A2A durable task claims, audit and
-learning ledgers, and tenant-local policy state still live in the mounted data
-root. Multiple dashboard/serve pods would split approval and idempotency
+Keep `replicaCount=1` and `autoscaling.enabled=false`. Postgres now centralizes
+the world model/approvals, governed Security/CRA/trust/evidence records, durable
+A2A claims, effect receipts, flow definitions/releases/runs, the promotion and
+rollback journal, hunter records/response claims/leases, the canonical audit
+append sequence, selected replay and revocation stores, dashboard settings and
+role overrides, configuration
+proposals, fleet roster, desired adapter release/health observations,
+calibration freeze state, conditional holdout query budgets, form submissions,
+and (when `[self_harness] store = "world"`) the harness learning subfamily.
+Holdout budgets additionally require one valid deployment-stable
+`self_harness.holdout_authority_id`; a secure Redis queue must be configured
+separately for network dispatch and poll leases. Postgres also
+centralizes tenant lifecycle/policy/KMS/accounting state, event/SaaS trigger
+registries, digest-only public-form routing and submission claims, learned-tool
+admissions, verifier confirmation claims, and desired verifier/correction/DGM
+artifact releases plus per-node health observations. Identity families join
+the shared plane only after deployment policy and external SCIM-secret digests
+are bound successfully.
+
+That is still not the whole authority plane. Audit private-key custody,
+channel-federation delivery/replay, in-process agent-bus delivery and
+acknowledgement, and work-discovery enrollment/lease/revocation state remain
+local. Learned artifact bytes are node-local even though shared desired-state
+and exact health observations gate serving. Offline approver keys and the fleet
+encryption key are immutable external trust inputs and block readiness until
+their deployment digests verify. Evaluator slots and callback-only learning
+rollouts have no built-in persistent authority and are reported as
+evidence-backed inactive families; their capability seams reactivate local
+blockers when implemented. Multiple dashboard/serve pods could still split
 authority. The chart fails closed on that topology; scale remote goal workers
-separately until every control-plane store has a shared transactional backend.
+separately until the authority report is green and a witnessed contention,
+failover, partition, and restore campaign proves the deployment.
+The [`production-assurance` reference](../reference-architectures/production-assurance/)
+therefore uses Postgres for strict shared effect/governed-record storage while
+remaining one control-plane replica until the residual authorities are shared
+and the full topology has deployment-specific failover/restore evidence.
 
