+### Backend cutovers and DSN rotation
+
+Treat the governed-record backend as a process-lifetime authority, not a live
+reload setting. After a process first resolves the selector, Lightwork pins the
+resolved backend, a non-secret SHA-256 digest of the complete DSN, and the
+enterprise/multi-replica enforcement floor. The first resolved selection is
+pinned even when it is local. A later local-to-Postgres or Postgres-to-local
+change, DSN change, or enforcement-floor change fails closed before another
+persistence plane can be used. One record operation also carries its initially
+resolved backend through audit-outbox delivery.
+
+For a cutover, rollback, endpoint change, or credential-bearing DSN rotation:
+
+1. stop admission and drain in-flight requests, workers, and scheduled jobs;
+2. stop every process that can read or mutate governed authority state;
+3. migrate/import and verify state, then archive any legacy local authority
+   files required by the relevant cutover check;
+4. update the backend/DSN/replica policy; and
+5. restart the complete control plane before reopening admission.
+
+For tenant-control cutover, the blocking legacy set includes the global
+`tenant_registry.json` and each tenant's `usage/ledger.json` and
+`usage/reservations.json`. Postgres stores one digest-addressed global namespace
+claim per tenant plus one tenant-scoped lifecycle/accounting CAS document.
+Incomplete provisioning claims are denied and omitted from normal roster reads;
+operators must explicitly resume or abort them. Shared deletion is tombstone-
+only at this layer. Synchronous `purge=True` is intentionally refused because a
+single pod cannot prove fleet/object-store erasure completion.
+
+The job queue additionally publishes a private
+`<legacy-db>.postgres-authority` marker while holding the same strict
+cross-process gate used by every current SQLite queue operation. The marker is
+bound to a SHA-256 digest of the selected DSN. A live or unrecognized legacy
+queue, poll marker, poll lock, mismatched marker, or mixed-version local writer
+fails the Postgres side closed. This fence closes the in-process handoff race;
+the full stop in step 2 remains mandatory for old binaries that predate the
+fence or for non-Lightwork programs that write the SQLite file directly.
+Production queue construction is admitted only through that deployment
+selector while its cutover gate is held: an explicit DSN cannot bypass the
+selector token, schema/sealing checks, legacy-path binding, or cutover marker
+protocol.
+
+Do not roll these settings through live replicas one at a time. If credentials
+must rotate without changing the DSN string, use a stable provider-side secret
+or connection indirection; changing the configured DSN requires the coordinated
+drain and restart above.
