+Effective identity authorization also moves as one unit. SCIM users/groups,
+pairwise OIDC-subject bindings, durable retirement tombstones, and recoverable
+tenant suspend/delete checkpoints share one encrypted deployment-global CAS
+document. Bootstrap admins, group role/suite maps, `default_role`,
+`default_suites`, and relevant auth flags are canonicalized and SHA-256-bound
+to that authority. The SCIM bearer remains external; only an order-independent
+digest of the configured bearer set is stored. Rotation requires the exact
+expected digest and may retain the previous set until a Postgres-clock grace
+deadline. During that grace, rolling replicas may expose the comma-separated
+union of the old and new sets; remove every old entry before the deadline because
+the union is rejected afterward. Policy drift, bearer drift, a backend outage,
+or the presence of any
+legacy `scim_users.json`, `scim_groups.json`, `oidc-subjects.json`,
+`oidc-retired-subjects.json`, `dashboard-users.json`, or
+`dashboard-user-suites.json` keeps all four identity families unpromoted and
+fails authorization closed. Deprovision first commits the inactive user and
+tombstones, then revokes sessions and applies tenant lifecycle changes through
+durable checkpoints; every lifecycle timestamp is selected inside the shared
+CAS retry from Postgres time. OIDC and SAML browser login likewise issue no
+session until the pairwise/transient-subject binding is durably committed and
+the resulting SCIM lifecycle status has been checked; local mode retains its
+historical best-effort binding behavior.
