+### Shared Postgres signer trust and rotation
+
+The canonical Postgres audit authority never uses node-local `.pub`, `.key`, or
+`.injected` files as historical trust. All replicas read the same public-key
+epochs from Postgres, rooted in an operator-controlled digest configured
+identically on every node:
+
+```toml
+[audit]
+sign = true
+require_offhost_key = true
+trust_root_sha256 = "sha256:<64-lowercase-hex>"
+```
+
+Private keys remain in KMS/secret-manager custody and are never written to the
+trust tables. Bootstrap and rotation are explicit, fail-closed ceremonies:
+
+```bash
+# Compute and pin this before bootstrap on every replica.
+maverick audit trust-root-digest --public-key <epoch-1-public-hex>
+maverick audit trust-bootstrap
+
+# The current signer authorizes the next public half but remains active.
+maverick audit trust-rotate --next-public-key <next-public-hex>
+```
+
+After staging, deploy the matching next private key. Its first append proves
+possession and atomically activates the new signer epoch with that event and its
+tenant head. Old writers then fail stale. Prior public keys remain in the signed
+shared history for verification; `--revoke-previous` marks the predecessor
+revoked rather than normally retired. A database outage, root-pin mismatch,
+malformed transition, selector drift, or missing bootstrap refuses reads and
+writes instead of falling back to local trust.
+
+If a staged private key is lost or cannot sign, recover with the still-active
+signer and name the exact pending key being cancelled:
+
+```bash
+maverick audit trust-rotate \
+  --replace-pending-key-id <stranded-key-id> \
+  --next-public-key <replacement-public-hex>
+```
+
+This is an atomic compare-and-swap, not an unsigned administrative reset. The
+active key signs the replacement transition and a hash-chained recovery record
+that preserves both the cancelled and replacement authorizations. The recovery
+record, trust-state digest, and pending row change commit together; failure
+rolls all three back and leaves the prior pending attempt retryable. Reusing a
+cancelled or previously attempted public key is refused.
